WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.709 --> 00:00:08.797
I'm a Latinx son of immigrants
from a small Texas border town.

00:00:08.838 --> 00:00:11.299
Growing up, I didn’t have
access to classical music,

00:00:11.299 --> 00:00:13.468
and I had never been to the symphony.

00:00:13.510 --> 00:00:16.596
But I always knew that I wanted
to play a string instrument.

00:00:16.638 --> 00:00:17.889
So in sixth grade,

00:00:17.931 --> 00:00:21.101
we got the opportunity to pick
our instruments for orchestra class.

00:00:21.101 --> 00:00:24.437
I took one look at the viola
and said, "Great, that'll work."

00:00:26.481 --> 00:00:30.360
And as I began to play the instrument,
I immediately fell in love with it.

00:00:31.319 --> 00:00:33.613
Even though my middle school
orchestra director

00:00:33.613 --> 00:00:35.240
was a fabulous bass teacher,

00:00:35.240 --> 00:00:37.867
she could only teach me
so much on the viola.

00:00:37.867 --> 00:00:40.537
And because there weren't
any viola teachers in my town,

00:00:40.537 --> 00:00:43.081
to be able to play the things
that I heard in my head,

00:00:43.081 --> 00:00:46.084
I turned to a teacher
who seemed to know everything:

00:00:46.126 --> 00:00:47.377
YouTube.

00:00:47.419 --> 00:00:48.420
(Laughter)

00:00:48.461 --> 00:00:50.672
By watching countless hours
of YouTube videos

00:00:50.714 --> 00:00:53.550
of the great virtuosos
performing everything from Beethoven

00:00:53.592 --> 00:00:56.011
to the musical suite from "Star Wars,"

00:00:56.052 --> 00:00:57.721
I tried to mimic their techniques

00:00:57.762 --> 00:01:00.390
and incorporate them into my own playing.

00:01:01.266 --> 00:01:05.103
With a few difficult years of practice,
a lot of blood, sweat and tears

00:01:05.145 --> 00:01:08.607
and the endless support
of an entire community of people

00:01:08.607 --> 00:01:11.318
who really, really wanted me to succeed,

00:01:11.318 --> 00:01:14.654
I began to play in places
that I'd never even dreamed of,

00:01:14.654 --> 00:01:16.239
like Carnegie Hall,

00:01:16.239 --> 00:01:19.909
and bring my music to audiences
across four different continents.

00:01:19.909 --> 00:01:23.413
It had been one of the most difficult
learning experiences of my life,

00:01:23.413 --> 00:01:26.082
but one that proved an important point:

00:01:26.124 --> 00:01:29.169
anyone from anywhere
can do incredible things,

00:01:29.210 --> 00:01:33.173
if you connect them with the knowledge
and resources that they need to thrive.

00:01:33.673 --> 00:01:37.052
On my travels, I became friends
with the elite young musicians

00:01:37.093 --> 00:01:39.179
who seemed to have all the resources.

00:01:39.220 --> 00:01:41.264
Because they lived
next to musical capitals

00:01:41.306 --> 00:01:43.558
like New York, Chicago or LA,

00:01:43.558 --> 00:01:46.394
they studied at places like Juilliard
on the weekends.

00:01:46.394 --> 00:01:49.481
They watched their teachers
perform at major symphony orchestras.

00:01:49.481 --> 00:01:53.109
They had access to almost every
musical opportunity that you can imagine

00:01:53.109 --> 00:01:54.778
right in their home communities.

00:01:55.487 --> 00:01:58.239
And even though I was proud
of the hard work that I'd done

00:01:58.239 --> 00:01:59.532
to get to their level,

00:01:59.532 --> 00:02:01.368
I hated always feeling behind

00:02:01.368 --> 00:02:03.745
because I never had access
to private lessons.

00:02:04.371 --> 00:02:06.665
So you can imagine how excited I was

00:02:06.706 --> 00:02:10.085
when I had the opportunity to take
my first professional private lesson

00:02:10.126 --> 00:02:12.379
with one of the best violists
in the world.

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:15.882
I just knew he'd be so excited about
how much progress I'd made on my own

00:02:15.882 --> 00:02:18.927
and encourage me to become
a professional musician.

00:02:18.968 --> 00:02:22.263
So it came as such a surprise
when he told me to quit.

00:02:22.305 --> 00:02:24.099
(Laughter)

00:02:24.140 --> 00:02:27.644
He said kids like me
who didn't start playing at an early age,

00:02:27.686 --> 00:02:30.814
who didn't have access to private teachers
and the best resources,

00:02:30.814 --> 00:02:32.232
who didn't really understand

00:02:32.232 --> 00:02:34.818
how the classical music industry
really operated,

00:02:34.818 --> 00:02:37.987
generally could not make it
as professional musicians.

00:02:38.780 --> 00:02:41.533
Now, classical music
is incredibly competitive

00:02:41.533 --> 00:02:45.078
and sometimes people
with all the resources don't even make it.

00:02:45.078 --> 00:02:47.330
So I'm sure he thought
he was doing me a favor,

00:02:47.372 --> 00:02:50.542
and maybe he was, but I was shocked.

00:02:50.583 --> 00:02:52.043
It hurt.

00:02:52.085 --> 00:02:56.548
But I didn’t want to disrespect him,
so I smiled, said, “OK,” and left.

00:02:56.589 --> 00:03:00.176
I was a 15-year-old who loved
playing the viola more than anything,

00:03:00.218 --> 00:03:05.098
but in that moment I seriously considered
putting my instrument away forever.

00:03:05.140 --> 00:03:06.933
Now I'm 21 years old

00:03:06.975 --> 00:03:10.812
and I couldn't be more grateful to have
experienced that moment of discouragement

00:03:10.854 --> 00:03:12.689
because it opened my eyes to the fact

00:03:12.689 --> 00:03:15.567
that for 13 million children
in the United States

00:03:15.567 --> 00:03:17.902
who live outside of major US cities,

00:03:17.902 --> 00:03:22.073
pursuing a career in something
like classical music, ballet, art,

00:03:22.073 --> 00:03:25.660
research or many other
historically exclusive fields

00:03:25.660 --> 00:03:29.205
is incredibly more difficult,
if not practically impossible.

00:03:29.873 --> 00:03:32.417
In classical music, for example,

00:03:32.459 --> 00:03:36.838
people of color make up only
around 15 percent of America’s orchestras,

00:03:36.880 --> 00:03:41.134
despite representing almost
42 percent of the US population.

00:03:41.843 --> 00:03:46.222
So in some ways, I don't blame
the viola teacher for telling me to quit.

00:03:46.264 --> 00:03:50.268
Music opportunities
tend to cluster around major cities,

00:03:50.310 --> 00:03:54.230
especially the charities and nonprofits
that try to help underserved students.

00:03:54.230 --> 00:03:55.732
And I just wasn't around them.

00:03:56.232 --> 00:03:58.485
But people have talent everywhere,

00:03:58.485 --> 00:04:00.278
and everybody deserves a chance

00:04:00.278 --> 00:04:03.406
to grow that talent
and share it with the world.

00:04:03.406 --> 00:04:06.576
Because when we allow people like me
to systemically be prevented

00:04:06.576 --> 00:04:09.245
from entering into
these competitive fields,

00:04:09.245 --> 00:04:12.832
we uphold the elitist
and exclusionary systems

00:04:12.874 --> 00:04:14.834
that allow so many children to be told

00:04:14.876 --> 00:04:17.587
that they're not worthy enough
to pursue their dreams.

00:04:18.254 --> 00:04:19.881
I wanted to change that.

00:04:19.923 --> 00:04:22.717
For many years, I had no clue how.

00:04:22.759 --> 00:04:26.638
But when the COVID pandemic hit
and our entire world went virtual,

00:04:26.679 --> 00:04:29.808
I saw an opportunity
to help others learn music online,

00:04:29.849 --> 00:04:32.519
regardless of who they were,
where they lived,

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:33.937
or what they could afford.

00:04:34.562 --> 00:04:37.273
So I texted a few friends
I knew from around the country

00:04:37.273 --> 00:04:39.692
and put together a founding team.

00:04:39.692 --> 00:04:42.821
Together we reached out
to 20 other musicians that we knew

00:04:42.821 --> 00:04:46.366
and asked each of them to teach
a free virtual private lesson a week

00:04:46.366 --> 00:04:48.493
to an underserved young student.

00:04:49.077 --> 00:04:51.871
This meant that students like Samuel Igbo,

00:04:51.871 --> 00:04:55.250
a 14-year-old violinist
from a small town in Texas

00:04:55.291 --> 00:04:58.795
who dreams of pursuing a career
as a concert violinist,

00:04:58.837 --> 00:05:02.841
could receive personalized
musical mentorship from Nikki Naghavi,

00:05:02.882 --> 00:05:06.010
a prize-winning violinist
at the New England Conservatory.

00:05:06.761 --> 00:05:11.349
My idea inspired and connected
hundreds of people from all walks of life

00:05:11.391 --> 00:05:15.061
to offer their time and expertise
to aspiring musicians like Sam

00:05:15.103 --> 00:05:16.771
from around the country.

00:05:16.771 --> 00:05:20.108
And now, just two and a half years later,

00:05:20.108 --> 00:05:22.819
this little idea has turned
into Through the Staff,

00:05:22.819 --> 00:05:26.865
a national nonprofit
that has provided almost 600,000 dollars

00:05:26.865 --> 00:05:29.033
of free virtual private lessons

00:05:29.033 --> 00:05:30.952
to almost 1000 young musicians

00:05:30.952 --> 00:05:34.706
from Fresno, California
to Wilkesboro, North Carolina.

00:05:35.540 --> 00:05:40.128
I didn't realize it, but I'd accidentally
started a virtual nonprofit.

00:05:40.169 --> 00:05:43.006
We didn’t just use the internet
to deliver our services,

00:05:43.047 --> 00:05:46.509
our organization existed on the internet.

00:05:46.551 --> 00:05:49.304
Because our program
was run entirely by volunteers

00:05:49.345 --> 00:05:51.139
from around the country

00:05:51.180 --> 00:05:53.933
who work together for a common cause,

00:05:53.975 --> 00:05:56.144
despite never meeting once in person.

00:05:56.686 --> 00:05:59.939
Because our students and teachers
could learn from each other online,

00:05:59.939 --> 00:06:01.357
wherever they were,

00:06:01.357 --> 00:06:04.360
geographic boundaries no longer mattered.

00:06:04.360 --> 00:06:08.239
This digital workspace meant
virtually no startup or scaling costs.

00:06:08.239 --> 00:06:10.909
So we've been able to grow
and make incredible changes

00:06:10.909 --> 00:06:14.829
with a budget of only 9,000 dollars
over two and a half years.

00:06:15.455 --> 00:06:18.917
It was only after I began to see
this profound potential for impact

00:06:18.958 --> 00:06:21.669
that I realized that virtual organizations

00:06:21.711 --> 00:06:24.672
can help us better distribute
educational resources

00:06:24.714 --> 00:06:27.967
directly to those who would
benefit from them the most.

00:06:28.009 --> 00:06:30.303
And if this worked in classical music,

00:06:30.345 --> 00:06:32.805
I figured it can work
in other places, too.

00:06:32.847 --> 00:06:35.767
This time in academic research.

00:06:35.808 --> 00:06:37.936
When I began to explore
biological research

00:06:37.977 --> 00:06:39.938
in my second year of college,

00:06:39.938 --> 00:06:41.898
I remember trying to find a mentor

00:06:41.898 --> 00:06:47.111
and being discouraged
to only see white male researchers.

00:06:47.111 --> 00:06:50.990
Just like in music,
this trend is systemic.

00:06:50.990 --> 00:06:55.912
Only 15 percent of all doctoral
researchers were Native American,

00:06:55.912 --> 00:06:58.581
African-American or Hispanic.

00:06:58.623 --> 00:07:00.333
I wanted to change that.

00:07:00.375 --> 00:07:02.418
And I knew that
the virtual nonprofit model

00:07:02.460 --> 00:07:04.045
that I had already developed

00:07:04.087 --> 00:07:06.297
could make a difference here, too.

00:07:06.339 --> 00:07:10.802
I could start something that could help
generations of future young researchers

00:07:10.843 --> 00:07:12.428
break into academia.

00:07:13.179 --> 00:07:18.935
So in 2021, my best friend and I gathered
a founding team of 31 college students

00:07:18.977 --> 00:07:24.023
and started another virtual nonprofit
called the Pre-College Research Institute.

00:07:24.023 --> 00:07:28.152
Together, we've created
four 32-hour virtual research courses

00:07:28.152 --> 00:07:33.032
that have taught, trained and inspired
160 high school students of color

00:07:33.032 --> 00:07:35.118
from Klerksdorp, South Africa

00:07:35.118 --> 00:07:36.953
to New Delhi, India,

00:07:36.953 --> 00:07:38.204
to Alpharetta, Georgia.

00:07:39.288 --> 00:07:43.710
If I could break down barriers
in access to music and academic research,

00:07:43.751 --> 00:07:45.962
anyone can do it anywhere.

00:07:46.504 --> 00:07:49.966
That's exactly what makes
virtual nonprofits so powerful.

00:07:50.008 --> 00:07:52.176
They transcend geographic boundaries

00:07:52.218 --> 00:07:53.761
and bring opportunities to those

00:07:53.803 --> 00:07:56.639
who would literally never
otherwise have them.

00:07:56.681 --> 00:07:59.142
They're a new way
to find and connect the world

00:07:59.183 --> 00:08:01.811
to a huge source of untapped talent

00:08:01.853 --> 00:08:05.606
and a necessary step to nurturing
the future of these industries.

00:08:05.606 --> 00:08:10.236
This is the future that will not only
change the fabric of these institutions,

00:08:10.236 --> 00:08:13.906
but ensure they survive
and prosper for years to come.

00:08:14.991 --> 00:08:18.494
And as more and more children
are connected to the opportunities

00:08:18.494 --> 00:08:22.790
and knowledge and resources
that they need to chase their dreams,

00:08:22.832 --> 00:08:25.835
I'm excited to see the people
that lead every industry

00:08:25.877 --> 00:08:28.171
look a lot more
like the world around them.

00:08:28.212 --> 00:08:30.548
I know this transformation can happen,

00:08:30.590 --> 00:08:32.508
but I can't do it alone.

00:08:32.550 --> 00:08:34.802
So if you have an idea or interest

00:08:34.844 --> 00:08:38.473
for how to make a historically
inaccessible field more equitable,

00:08:38.514 --> 00:08:40.099
grab a few friends,

00:08:40.141 --> 00:08:43.561
brainstorm a way that you can break down
the barriers in your field

00:08:43.603 --> 00:08:45.646
and start a virtual nonprofit.

00:08:45.646 --> 00:08:48.608
You may never meet the majority
of the people that you serve,

00:08:48.608 --> 00:08:50.318
but the effects of your hard work

00:08:50.318 --> 00:08:53.321
will touch them in ways
that they will never forget.

00:08:53.321 --> 00:08:54.614
Thank you.

00:08:54.614 --> 00:08:55.865
(Applause)

